Found during the k108 rework (worker drift note, confirmed by the test-writer's measurement). Not a template-visible difference: JS has a tracing GC, the port frees by reference count.

Shape: main = {% set bag = [] %}{% include 'i.njk' %}; i.njk = {% macro m() %}[{{ x | later }}]{% endmacro %}{% set _ = bag.push(m) %}{{ y | later }} where 'later' is an async filter that parks its callback and the application drops it without answering (an abandoned render). After the callbacks are dropped one Context is still alive (HEAD 04e3a23: two). Also with the include inside an if. Path: includer -> shared array -> macro -> its callback -> render callback -> include continuation -> includer. A macro kept inside the included template only (not handed to the includer) is freed (0 live).

Possible direction (worker's note): make the include continuation and the waterfall's final continuation plain (not Context-holding) and let the included Context's hold on its includer carry it; needs its own audit.

Pinned as a TODO in t/10-leaks.t (subtest 'an included render whose async filter never answers'). Related: k108.

fidelity-auditor, found during the k108 re-review: this card's path is wider than its title, in two ways. (1) The included render need not be abandoned -- it is enough that it FAILS after handing the macro up; the render then completes with the error callback and one Context (the includer's) stays for good. (2) The macro need not contain an async construct -- a plain macro defined after one in the included template (after an import, a from-import, an include, an async filter) reaches the callback the same way. Minimal: main = {% set bag = [] %}A{% include 'i.njk' %}B ; i.njk = {% include 'p.njk' %}{% macro m() %}m{% endmacro %}{% set _ = bag.push(m) %}{% include 'missing.njk' %} -> 1 Context, 2 Frames alive after the error callback (HEAD 04e3a23: 2 Contexts); same with {% from 'lib.njk' import h %} in place of the first include, and with {{ nope() }} as the error. A plain macro with nothing async before it: 0. Same path as described above; on the error path the include's waterfall-final continuation is never called (upstream: if(err) { cb(err); return; }) and keeps the includer. The error-path variant has no TODO pin yet. In a randomized stress of interleaved renders this class is what remains (18-25 Contexts per 150 renders) once the k108 error-path finding is fixed.

2026-11-17 11:58 fidelity-auditor, third k108 look (tree with 'undef $callback;' in the include's error branch). This replaces part of my earlier note here: the top-level and in-an-if error shapes I listed are closed by that statement (0 live). What remains, measured on 4640 failure shapes against real upstream (396 leak, the tree never more than HEAD: 252 fewer, 144 equal):
(1) No hand-up needed. An included template that made a closure reaching its callback (macro with an async construct, plain macro defined after an include or import, import of such a library; a plain macro alone: 0) and then fails leaves 2-3 Contexts when the include runs in an asyncEach / asyncAll body -- or in a plain for whose body has an async filter, which the transformer turns into asyncEach -- after a deferral in that body. Minimal: main = {% for r in [1,2] %}{{ y | later }}{% include 'i.njk' %}{% endfor %} ; i.njk = {% macro m(a) %}[{{ a | later }}]{% endmacro %}{% include 'missing.njk' %} ('later' parks its callback, the queue is answered one by one) -> 2 Contexts, 3 Frames after the error callback; asyncEach 2, asyncAll 3; HEAD 04e3a23 the same. Without a deferral before the include, or with if / top level in place of the loop: 0 -- the test-writer's correction (a deferral or deferring sibling is needed) is confirmed.
(2) Path: the include's render callback -> the continuation it is nested in (a Perl closure keeps the closure instance it was made in, and with it that instance's captures) -> the loop's next -> the loop's final continuation, never called after an error -> includer -> kept included Context -> macro -> its callback -> render callback. 'undef $callback;' cuts the waterfall step, not this enclosing-closure link.
(3) Import variant: a library imported 'with context' that pushes a macro into an array of the importer and fails inside asyncEach / asyncAll leaves 1 Context, no deferral needed.
(4) If k108's finding A is fixed by keeping a failed library (auditor's option 1 there), a failing import inside asyncEach / asyncAll leaks as at HEAD again (444 matrix shapes) until this card closes that path.
No TODO pins (1) or (3) yet: the two TODO groups in t/10-leaks.t and t/10-include-failure.t cover only the hand-up shape. The title and the ADR 0021 / POD sentences that quote it ('hands a macro ... to the includer') are narrower than the leak.

2026-11-17 12:39 fidelity-auditor, fourth k108 look -- CORRECTION of my note above. 'HEAD 04e3a23 the same' and 'the tree never more than HEAD' hold only for macros that read an outer name (all my earlier shapes did; there HEAD leaks through the macro itself). The minimal shape as I typed it has a macro that reads only its argument, and HEAD frees that. Measured: main = {% for r in [1,2] %}{{ y | later }}{% include 'i.njk' %}{% endfor %} ; i.njk = {% macro m(a) %}[{{ a | later }}]{% endmacro %}{% include 'missing.njk' %} -> tree 2 Contexts 3 Frames, HEAD 0, first k108 tree 0; with {{ x }} added to the macro body HEAD has 2 and 3 too. Same in asyncEach after a deferral and in asyncAll with a deferring sibling (asyncAll, sibling defers, i.njk = {% macro m(a) %}[{{ a | af }}]{% endmacro %}{% include 'missing.njk' %}: tree 2 and 3, HEAD 0). On a 4640-shape failure matrix with every outer read removed from the macro bodies: 42 include-site shapes leak more than HEAD (asyncAll with a deferring sibling, macro with an async filter; 28 of them from nothing), import sites none, 3282 shapes leak less than HEAD. So this card is, in that sub-shape, a leak k108 introduced (include rule B plus the take-over when the included render delivers an error), not only one it left. Pinned as TODO K3 in t/10-leaks.t; the asyncAll-with-sibling and asyncEach variants with an argument-only macro have no pin of their own. Point (4) of my note happened: k108 keeps a failed library, a failing import inside asyncEach / asyncAll leaks as at HEAD (684 of 3040 import-site shapes, each with HEAD's counts; K1, K2, K4). Suggestion: priority above low.

